\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a group $H$, its derived subgroup $H'=[H,H]$ is generated by all commutators $x^{-1}y^{-1}xy$. A finite group $G$ is integrable if there exists a finite group $H$ with $H'\cong G$. A finite multiplication table is one explicit encoding of the input group; any proposed complexity bound must specify its chosen encoding and size parameter.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there an algorithm that, on every input finite group $G$, terminates and correctly decides whether some finite group $H$ satisfies $H'\cong G$? If such a decision algorithm exists, determine the complexity of this decision problem, with the input representation and size measure made explicit. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18829v1}{[S3]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Decide whether a finite group occurs as the commutator subgroup of a finite group, and determine the computational complexity of doing so.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2541 (KOU-21.32). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] P. J. Cameron, Problem 21.32, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records decidability and a complexity result; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\item[S3] Sathasivam Kalithasan and Viji Z. Thomas, \emph{On the decidability of the integrability of finite groups}, arXiv:2602.18829v1 (2026), Introduction, main theorem and corollary. It proves decidability by a computable finite order bound. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18829v1}.
\end{description}
